+---
+id: lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities
+kind: lemma
+title: "Equivariant Thom-Gysin Identities for Fixed Submanifolds"
+status: draft
+origin: pipeline
+domain: mathematics
+library: mathematics
+dependency_level: 2
+deps:
+  - def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions
+dependency_roles:
+  def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions: mathematical-premise
+proof_strategy: direct
+provenance:
+  statement: literature-derived
+  proof: ai-generated
+sources:
+  scraped: []
+  references:
+    - title: "Michael F. Atiyah and Raoul Bott, The Moment Map and Equivariant Cohomology"
+      url: "https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf"
+    - title: "Matvei Libine, Lecture Notes on Equivariant Cohomology"
+      url: "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0709.3615"
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Let $T$ be a compact torus acting smoothly on a compact oriented smooth manifold $M$ without boundary. Let $N\subseteq M^T$ be a compact closed submanifold of codimension $c$, let $i:N\hookrightarrow M$ be inclusion, and let its normal bundle $\nu$ have a $T$-invariant orientation. Orient $N$ by $o(TM|_N)=o(TN)\wedge o(\nu)$. With the rational Borel conventions of the preceding definition, there is a fiber-normalized equivariant Thom class $\tau_\nu\in H_T^c(\nu,\nu\setminus N;\mathbb Q)$, and cup product with $\tau_\nu$ is an isomorphism $H_T^k(N;\mathbb Q)\to H_T^{k+c}(\nu,\nu\setminus N;\mathbb Q)$ for every $k$. A $T$-invariant tubular neighborhood and excision therefore define a cohomological Gysin map $i_*^T:H_T^k(N;\mathbb Q)\to H_T^{k+c}(M;\mathbb Q)$. It satisfies
+
+$$i^*i_*^T(\beta)=e_T(\nu)\smile\beta,\qquad \int_M i_*^T(\beta)=\int_N\beta.$$
+
+If $N'$ is a different fixed component disjoint from $N$, then $i_{N'}^*i_{N*}^T(\beta)=0$. These maps use the Thom normalization in which every oriented normal fiber integrates to $1$.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Atiyah and Bott, §2 and §3, especially the Thom pushforward and equation (3.7)](https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf)
+
+[Libine, §7.7, PDF pp. 41–42](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0709.3615)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** A compact torus $T$, a compact oriented smooth $T$-manifold $M$ without boundary, a compact closed $T$-fixed submanifold $N$, and a $T$-invariant orientation of its normal bundle, as in the Statement.
+
+[A1] The inclusion $i:N\hookrightarrow M$ has an oriented normal bundle $\nu$ of rank $c$; $T$ acts trivially on $N$ and linearly on the fibers of $\nu$.
+
+[F1] [[def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions]]: $H_T^*(X;\mathbb Q)=H^*(ET\times_T X;\mathbb Q)$, equivariant integration is the degree-shifting pushforward of the Borel fibration, and the Euler class is the zero-section pullback of a Thom class normalized to integrate to $1$ on each oriented fiber.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 Average any Riemannian metric on $M$ over normalized Haar measure on $T$ to obtain a $T$-invariant metric. The normal exponential map is $T$-equivariant; its derivative is invertible along the zero section, so the inverse function theorem and compactness of $N$ give a $T$-invariant tubular neighborhood identified with $\nu$. Since $T$ fixes $N$ pointwise, $ET\times_T N\cong BT\times N$, and the Borel normal bundle $ET\times_T\nu$ is an oriented rank-$c$ vector bundle over the CW space $BT\times N$. [given, A1, F1, construct]
+
+1.2 Filter $BT\times N$ by its CW skeleta. Over each open $p$-cell the normal bundle is trivial, and the associated relative disk/sphere pair over that cell has quotient $D^p\times D^c/((\partial D^p\times D^c)\cup(D^p\times S^{c-1}))\cong S^{p+c}$. Its relative cohomology is one copy of $\mathbb Q$ in degree $p+c$, generated by the base-cell orientation times the chosen fiber orientation. Orientation-preserving transition maps preserve this generator, so the cellular cochain complex of the Thom pair is the cellular cochain complex of $BT\times N$ shifted upward by $c$. The compatible fiber generators therefore give a Thom class $\tau_\nu$, and cup product with it is an isomorphism in every degree. [given, A1, step 1.1, construct, algebra]
+
+1.3 Transfer the Thom class through the tubular-neighborhood identification and excision to $H_T^c(M,M\setminus N;\mathbb Q)$. The natural map from relative to absolute cohomology sends $\tau_\nu\smile p^*\beta$ to a uniquely defined $i_*^T(\beta)$; the Thom isomorphism proves this construction is defined for every $\beta\in H_T^*(N;\mathbb Q)$. [given, F1, step 1.1, step 1.2, construct]
+
+2.1 Pull the relative class in step 1.3 back along the zero section. By the definition of $e_T(\nu)$ in [F1], the pulled-back Thom class is $e_T(\nu)$, and pullback respects cup products. Hence $i^*i_*^T(\beta)=e_T(\nu)\smile\beta$. [given, F1, step 1.3, algebra]
+
+2.2 The Gysin class in step 1.3 is represented in a tubular neighborhood of $N$. Choose such neighborhoods disjoint for disjoint compact fixed components $N,N'$. The representative restricts to zero on $N'$, so $i_{N'}^*i_{N*}^T(\beta)=0$. [given, step 1.1, step 1.3]
+
+3.1 In the tubular-neighborhood representative from step 1.3, integrate first along each oriented normal disk fiber. The Thom class integrates to $1$ by its fiber normalization, so this fiber integration returns $\beta$ on $N$; the orientation convention in the Statement makes the resulting base orientation the one used by $\int_N$. [given, F1, step 1.3, algebra]
+
+3.2 Equivariant integration over $M$ factors in the tubular neighborhood into integration over the normal fibers followed by integration over $N$. Step 3.1 therefore gives $\int_M i_*^T(\beta)=\int_N\beta$. Steps 2.1–3.2 establish all stated identities. [given, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎
