+---
+id: lem-abbv-fixed-component-euler-invertibility
+kind: lemma
+title: "Fixed-Component Normal Euler Classes Become Invertible"
+status: draft
+origin: pipeline
+domain: mathematics
+library: mathematics
+dependency_level: 3
+deps:
+  - def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions
+  - lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities
+dependency_roles:
+  def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions: mathematical-premise
+  lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities: mathematical-premise
+proof_strategy: direct
+provenance:
+  statement: literature-derived
+  proof: ai-generated
+sources:
+  scraped: []
+  references:
+    - title: "Michael F. Atiyah and Raoul Bott, The Moment Map and Equivariant Cohomology"
+      url: "https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf"
+    - title: "Matvei Libine, Lecture Notes on Equivariant Cohomology"
+      url: "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0709.3615"
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Let a compact torus $T$ act smoothly on a compact smooth manifold $M$ without boundary, and let $F=M^T$. Then $F$ is a compact smooth embedded submanifold, its connected components $F_j$ are finite in number, and $T_pF=(T_pM)^T$ for every $p\in F$. The normal bundle $\nu_j$ of each component has no trivial $T$-representation in any fiber. Give $\nu_j$ a $T$-equivariant orientation and choose an invariant complex structure on it; let $\epsilon_j\in\{+1,-1\}$ compare that complex orientation with the chosen orientation. If the nonzero characters of the normal representation on a fiber, counted with multiplicity, are $\chi_{j,1},\ldots,\chi_{j,c_j/2}$, then the component of $e_T(\nu_j)$ in $H^0(F_j;\mathbb Q)\otimes R$ is
+
+$$\epsilon_j\prod_{a=1}^{c_j/2}u_{\chi_{j,a}}.$$
+
+Consequently $e_T(\nu_j)$ is invertible in $S^{-1}H_T^*(F_j;\mathbb Q)$. When $c_j=0$, the product is empty, equals $1$, and the Euler class is already a unit.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Atiyah and Bott, §3, printed pp. 8–9, equations (3.7) and following argument](https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf)
+
+[Libine, §7.7, PDF pp. 42–43](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0709.3615)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** A compact torus $T$ acting smoothly on compact $M$ and the equivariant normal-bundle orientations and Thom/Euler conventions of the Statement.
+
+[A1] $F=M^T$; each connected component $F_j$ has compact finite-dimensional cohomology, and $\nu_j$ is its $T$-equivariant normal bundle.
+
+[F1] [[def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions]]: $R=H^*(BT;\mathbb Q)$, $S$ is generated by the nonzero character classes $u_\chi$, and $e_T(E)$ is the zero-section pullback of a fiber-normalized Borel Thom class.
+
+[F2] [[lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities]]: every oriented normal bundle of a compact $T$-fixed submanifold has a fiber-normalized Borel Thom class; its zero-section pullback is the equivariant Euler class.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 Average a Riemannian metric over normalized Haar measure on $T$. At $p\in F$, the exponential map of this invariant metric is $T$-equivariant and is injective on a sufficiently small ball in $T_pM$. Thus, for $v$ in that ball, $\exp_p(v)$ is fixed by every $t\in T$ exactly when $v$ is fixed by every linear map $d_pt$; the fixed set near $p$ is the exponential image of $(T_pM)^T$. Therefore $F$ is locally a smooth submanifold and $T_pF=(T_pM)^T$. [given, A1, construct]
+
+1.2 The orthogonal complement of $(T_pM)^T$ in $T_pM$ is the normal fiber, so it contains no $T$-fixed vector. A real representation of a connected compact torus splits into its trivial subspace and real two-dimensional character subspaces; the normal has only the latter. The character projections are obtained by averaging the representation against each character, so they vary smoothly along a fixed component and give its normal bundle's weight subbundles. [given, step 1.1, step 1.1]
+
+1.3 On each nontrivial real character subbundle, the torus representation supplies an invariant complex structure. Reversing one complex line orientation when necessary makes the direct-sum complex orientation compare to the chosen normal orientation by the sign $\epsilon_j$. Since $F_j$ is connected, the character multiplicities are constant on it. [given, A1, step 1.2, construct]
+
+2.1 The torus acts trivially on $F_j$, hence $ET\times_TF_j\cong BT\times F_j$ and the Künneth isomorphism over $\mathbb Q$ gives $H_T^*(F_j;\mathbb Q)=H^*(F_j;\mathbb Q)\otimes R$. Restrict the Euler class to a point of $F_j$. The normal representation is the direct sum of the complex character lines in step 1.3; their Borel Euler classes are $u_{\chi_{j,a}}$ by [F1], so multiplicativity gives the degree-zero-in-$H^*(F_j)$ component $f_j=\epsilon_j\prod_a u_{\chi_{j,a}}$. Every factor is nonzero and belongs to $S$, so $f_j$ is a unit in $S^{-1}R$. [given, F1, F2, step 1.3, algebra]
+
+2.2 Write $e_T(\nu_j)=f_j+n_j$, where $n_j$ has positive degree in $H^*(F_j;\mathbb Q)$. Since $F_j$ is compact of finite dimension, every product of sufficiently many positive-degree cohomology classes vanishes; choose $N$ with $n_j^N=0$. Then in the localized ring
+
+$$e_T(\nu_j)^{-1}=f_j^{-1}\sum_{q=0}^{N-1}(-f_j^{-1}n_j)^q,$$
+
+as multiplication by $1+f_j^{-1}n_j$ telescopes to $1-(-f_j^{-1}n_j)^N=1$. If $c_j=0$, $n_j=0$ and $f_j=1$, giving the empty-product case as well. [given, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
