+# Batch 9 — Step 1 authoring evidence
+
+Run: `advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23`  
+Batch: 9 (`beta`, `quantum-field-theory-mathematics`)
+
+## Plan, design, and dependency inventory
+
+The complete assigned design section, `research/plan-advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry-track.md` under “Atiyah–Bott–Berline–Vergne Integration Formula,” matches `research/plan-spec.json` and `research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-9.pages.json`: A page `qftx-abbv-integration`, B page `qftx-abbv-integration-examples`, title, order 965, category `quantum-field-theory-mathematics`, library `mathematics`, and the A-page prerequisite `qftx-borel-localization` agree. The design's scope is the compact oriented fixed-component pushforward formula with normal Euler-class denominators and the isolated-fixed-point specialization. It explicitly excludes arbitrary noncompact instanton spaces and infinite-dimensional field spaces. The plan's empty item arrays are planning placeholders. No design/plan conflict was found; the shared plan and proposed inventory remain unchanged.
+
+The full-run dependency-level record gives batch 8 level 1 with prerequisite batch 7 and batch 9 level 2 with prerequisite batch 8. The assigned manifest requires `qftx-borel-localization`; batch 8's planned item supplier `thm-borel-localization-for-compact-torus-actions` has planned `proposed_deps: []` (item level 0), so its planned theorem level is 1. The exact Borel theorem contract in `research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/scaffold/proposed-inventory.json` is: for a compact smooth torus manifold with rational or real coefficients, localize by the multiplicative set generated by nonzero torus characters and prove that restriction to the fixed locus becomes an isomorphism, with precise compactness/finite-type hypotheses. The same inventory gives `def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model` as a planned Borel/Cartan definition with `proposed_deps: []`, item level 0. Its Borel portion is used below; no Cartan comparison is assumed.
+
+Before any escalation disposition, `loadStep1Record(process.cwd(), run)` and `step1Decision(snapshot, "thm-borel-localization-for-compact-torus-actions")` were called. The result is `closed: false`, `reason: "Readiness record missing"`; the planned supplier has no current local item carrier or readiness row. This is recorded as an actual unresolved in-run prerequisite, not inferred from its `proposed-future-only` label alone. Initial dependency levels are being calculated from the item edges before each consumer is written; they will be finalized from the completed manifest below.
+
+## Source retrieval and reading
+
+Two independent full-text treatments were inspected. `source-fetch-check --stamp` passed 2/2 sources with no documented drops or retrieval failures. Exact hashes, byte counts and timestamps are in `batch-9.coverage.json`.
+
+- M. F. Atiyah and R. Bott, “The Moment Map and Equivariant Cohomology,” complete 28-page PDF, `https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf`. Read §2 equations (2.1)–(2.9), including the Borel construction and module structure, and §3, Theorem 3.5 through equations (3.7)–(3.8), including the support/localization argument, Thom-Gysin self-intersection, componentwise normal-weight Euler inversion, and pushforward derivation. The paper's introduction explicitly limits the proof to finite-dimensional manifolds and says it does not directly apply to infinite-dimensional functional integrals.
+- Matvei Libine, “Lecture Notes on Equivariant Cohomology,” complete 72-page graduate lecture notes, `https://arxiv.org/pdf/0709.3615`. Read §2.1 Definition 3, §5.3 on the equivariant Euler class, and §7.7 Theorems 69–71, PDF pp. 41–44, including the fixed-component pushforward, self-intersection, weight-product leading term, localized inverse, and integration argument. The notes' differential-form formula in §7.1–7.3 has explicit `(2π)` factors; the authored items use Borel cohomology and a Thom class integrating to 1 on each oriented fiber, so no extra factor appears. The §7.7 cohomological argument is the supporting treatment used. Its own introductory note says it follows Atiyah–Bott, Berline–Vergne, and Guillemin–Sternberg; it is recorded as an independently authored full lecture exposition, not as an independent source of theorem priority.
+
+Each harvested source result has an `included` or `context-only` disposition and exact item mapping in `batch-9.coverage.json`. The cited texts support conventions and the proof route; the mathematical arguments remain in the local carriers. No retrieval failed, so `source_resolution` has no failure/drop record.
+
+## Authored items
+
+### `def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions`
+
+Defines rational Borel equivariant cohomology, the coefficient ring and degree-two character normalization, the localization multiplicative set, fiber integration and its degree shift, the normalized Thom/Euler convention, and the fixed-component orientation convention. It uses only the Borel part of `def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model`; the Cartan comparison is outside its claim. Direct dependency: that exact planned definition, role `mathematical-premise`; planned supplier level 0 gives local item level 1. The full local statement and source references are in `items/def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions.md`.
+
+Current proof/status boundary: the planned definition has no current carrier; its live Step 1 readiness is missing. The local conventions are complete, but the definition's readiness will remain escalated until the real upstream carrier exists and its Borel interface matches the use recorded here.
+
+### `lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities`
+
