## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** AC, a compact torus $T$, a compact oriented smooth $T$-manifold $M$ without boundary, a compact closed $T$-fixed submanifold $N$, and a $T$-invariant orientation of its normal bundle, as in the Statement.

[A1] Assume AC. It is carried through the real Borel coefficient-ring supplier used in the preceding definition. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]

[A2] The inclusion $i:N\hookrightarrow M$ has an oriented normal bundle $\nu$ of rank $c$; $T$ acts trivially on $N$ and linearly on the fibers of $\nu$.

[F1] [[def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions]]: $H_T^*(X;\mathbb R)=H^*(ET\times_T X;\mathbb R)$, equivariant integration is the degree-shifting pushforward of the Borel fibration, and the Euler class is the zero-section pullback of a Thom class normalized to integrate to $1$ on each oriented fiber.

[F2] [[lem-qftx-borel-base-is-paracompact-of-cw-type]]: for the selected product Milnor model and compact smooth $N$, $BT\times N$ is paracompact Hausdorff and has CW type. This current batch-8 item is not inventoried and has no current Step 1 readiness record, so its use here remains conditional.

[F3] [[thm-milnor-join-model-is-a-contractible-free-g-space]]: each selected circle Milnor bundle is numerable; a finite product of its trivializing covers and subordinate numerations gives a numerable principal $T$-bundle, and pullback preserves numerability.

[F4] [[thm-thom-isomorphism-for-oriented-vector-bundles]]: under AC, an oriented numerable rank-$c$ vector bundle over a paracompact Hausdorff base of CW type has a fiber-normalized Thom class, and cup product with it is an isomorphism in every degree.

## Proof

1.1 Average any Riemannian metric on $M$ over normalized Haar measure on $T$ to obtain a $T$-invariant metric. The normal exponential map is $T$-equivariant; its derivative is invertible along the zero section, so the inverse function theorem and compactness of $N$ give a $T$-invariant tubular neighborhood identified with $\nu$. Since $T$ fixes $N$ pointwise, $ET\times_T N\cong BT\times N$, and the Borel normal bundle $ET\times_T\nu$ is an oriented rank-$c$ vector bundle over the CW space $BT\times N$. [given, A2, F1, construct]

1.2 The Borel principal bundle $ET\times N\to BT\times N$ is the pullback of the selected product Milnor bundle. If $r=0$, this is the trivial principal bundle over a point; otherwise, for each circle factor, [F3] supplies a numerable trivializing cover and subordinate partition. Products of these finitely many covers and partition functions are locally finite and subordinate to product principal-$T$ charts. Pulling the numeration back to $BT\times N$ shows that the associated Borel normal bundle $ET\times_T\nu$ is numerable. It is oriented by the given $T$-invariant orientation. By [F2] its base is paracompact Hausdorff of CW type, so [F4] gives the normalized Thom class $\tau_\nu$ and the cup-product isomorphism in every degree. [given, A2, F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, construct]

1.3 Use the tubular-neighborhood identification and excision to transfer $\tau_\nu$ to $H_T^c(M,M\setminus N;\mathbb R)$. For each $\beta\in H_T^*(N;\mathbb R)$, define $i_*^T(\beta)$ as the image of the cup product of this relative Thom class with the pullback of $\beta$ under the relative-to-absolute map $H_T^*(M,M\setminus N;\mathbb R)\to H_T^*(M;\mathbb R)$. This canonical composite is the Gysin map and is defined for every $\beta$ by the Thom isomorphism. [given, F1, step 1.1, step 1.2, construct]

2.1 Pull the relative class in step 1.3 back along the zero section. By the definition of $e_T(\nu)$ in [F1], the pulled-back Thom class is $e_T(\nu)$, and pullback respects cup products. Hence $i^*i_*^T(\beta)=e_T(\nu)\smile\beta$. [given, A1, F1, step 1.3, algebra]

2.2 The Gysin class in step 1.3 is represented in a tubular neighborhood of $N$. Choose such neighborhoods disjoint for disjoint compact fixed components $N,N'$. The representative restricts to zero on $N'$, so $i_{N'}^*i_{N*}^T(\beta)=0$. [given, step 1.1, step 1.3]

3.1 In the tubular-neighborhood representative from step 1.3, integrate first along each oriented normal disk fiber. The Thom class integrates to $1$ by its fiber normalization, so this fiber integration returns $\beta$ on $N$; the orientation convention in the Statement makes the resulting base orientation the one used by $\int_N$. [given, A1, F1, step 1.3, algebra]

3.2 Equivariant integration over $M$ factors in the tubular neighborhood into integration over the normal fibers followed by integration over $N$. Step 3.1 therefore gives $\int_M i_*^T(\beta)=\int_N\beta$. These identities establish every stated Gysin property. [given, A1, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎
## Proof

1.1 Average a Riemannian metric over normalized Haar measure on $T$. At $p\in F$, the exponential map of this invariant metric is $T$-equivariant and is injective on a sufficiently small ball in $T_pM$. Thus, for $v$ in that ball, $\exp_p(v)$ is fixed by every $t\in T$ exactly when $v$ is fixed by every linear map $d_pt$; the fixed set near $p$ is the exponential image of $(T_pM)^T$. Therefore $F$ is locally a smooth submanifold and $T_pF=(T_pM)^T$. The fixed locus is closed in compact $M$, hence compact; its connected components are open in this locally connected manifold and compactness gives only finitely many. [given, A1, A2, construct]

1.2 The orthogonal complement of $(T_pM)^T$ in $T_pM$ is the normal fiber, so it contains no $T$-fixed vector. The complexification of an orthogonal representation of the abelian group $T$ is a commuting family of unitary maps and decomposes into simultaneous character eigenspaces; complex conjugation pairs the $\chi$ and $\bar\chi$ spaces. A real one-dimensional character would have image in $\{+1,-1\}$ and is trivial because $T$ is connected. Thus every nontrivial real summand is two-dimensional. Averaging the representation against each character gives smooth projections onto the finitely many weight subbundles along each $F_j$. [given, A1, step 1.1, construct]

1.3 If $\nu_j$ has nonzero weights, choose $X\in\mathfrak t$ outside the finite union of their kernels. For a character $\chi$, define the real linear weight $\omega_\chi(X)$ by $\chi(\exp(sX))=\exp(2\pi i s\omega_\chi(X))$. On its real weight subbundle the infinitesimal action $A_X$ satisfies $A_X^2=-(2\pi\omega_\chi(X))^2I$, so $J_\chi=A_X/(2\pi\omega_\chi(X))$ is invariant and satisfies $J_\chi^2=-I$. Their direct sum gives an invariant complex structure on $\nu_j$. Its complex orientation may agree or disagree with the chosen normal orientation, with comparison sign $\epsilon_j$. The weight projections vary continuously, so their ranks, and therefore the character multiplicities, are constant on connected $F_j$. If there are no weights, then $\nu_j=0$ and the complex structure is vacuous. [given, A1, A2, step 1.2, construct]

2.1 The torus acts trivially on $F_j$, hence $ET\times_TF_j\cong BT\times F_j$ and the Künneth isomorphism over $\mathbb R$ gives $H_T^*(F_j;\mathbb R)=H^*(F_j;\mathbb R)\otimes\mathcal R$. Restrict $e_T(\nu_j)$ to any point of $F_j$. The normal representation is the direct sum of the complex character lines in step 1.3; the Euler class of a direct sum is the product because the product of the normalized Thom classes restricts to the positive generator on each direct-sum fiber and hence is its normalized Thom class. By [F1] each line has Euler class $u_{\chi_{j,a}}$, and the orientation comparison gives $f_j=\epsilon_j\prod_a u_{\chi_{j,a}}$ as the degree-zero-in-$H^*(F_j)$ component. Every factor is nonzero and belongs to $S$, so $f_j$ is a unit in $S^{-1}\mathcal R$. [given, A1, F1, F2, step 1.3, algebra]

2.2 Write $e_T(\nu_j)=f_j+n_j$, where $n_j$ has positive degree in $H^*(F_j;\mathbb R)$. Since $F_j$ is compact of finite dimension, every product of sufficiently many positive-degree cohomology classes vanishes; choose $N$ with $n_j^N=0$. Then in the localized ring

$$e_T(\nu_j)^{-1}=f_j^{-1}\sum_{q=0}^{N-1}(-f_j^{-1}n_j)^q,$$

as multiplication by $1+f_j^{-1}n_j$ telescopes to $1-(-f_j^{-1}n_j)^N=1$. If $c_j=0$, $n_j=0$ and $f_j=1$, giving the empty-product case as well. [given, A1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
## Proof

1.1 Localize the restriction map $i^*:H_T^*(M;\mathbb R)\to H_T^*(F;\mathbb R)$ at $S$. By [L2] it is an isomorphism in this compact smooth finite-type case. Let $\beta=i^*\alpha=(\beta_j)_j$ under $H_T^*(F)=\bigoplus_jH_T^*(F_j)$. [given, A1, L2]

1.2 By [F3], each $e_T(\nu_j)$ is invertible after localization. Form $\gamma=\sum_j i_{j*}^T(\beta_j/e_T(\nu_j))\in S^{-1}H_T^*(M;\mathbb R)$; the sum is finite because $F$ has finitely many components. [given, A1, F3, step 1.1, construct]

1.3 All maps and identities in [F2] are $\mathcal R$-linear and extend $S^{-1}\mathcal R$-linearly to the localized modules. For a component $F_\ell$, pull back $\gamma$. The $j=\ell$ term is $e_T(\nu_\ell)\,\beta_\ell/e_T(\nu_\ell)=\beta_\ell$ by the localized self-intersection identity; each $j\ne\ell$ term restricts to zero by the localized disjoint-support identity. Hence $i^*\gamma=\beta=i^*\alpha$. Since the localized restriction map in step 1.1 is injective, $\gamma=\alpha$. [given, F2, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]

2.1 The $\mathcal R$-linear pushforwards in [F1] and the identity $\int_M i_{j*}^T(\delta)=\int_{F_j}\delta$ in [F2] extend $S^{-1}\mathcal R$-linearly, so they apply to $\delta=\beta_j/e_T(\nu_j)$. Applying $\int_M$ to step 1.3 gives

$$\int_M\alpha=\sum_j\int_Mi_{j*}^T(\beta_j/e_T(\nu_j))=\sum_j\int_{F_j}\beta_j/e_T(\nu_j),$$

which is the fixed-component formula in the localized coefficient ring. If $F=\varnothing$, [L2] says $S^{-1}H_T^*(M)=0$, so $\int_M\alpha$ localizes to zero; because $\mathcal R$ is a domain and every element of $S$ is nonzero, $\mathcal R\hookrightarrow S^{-1}\mathcal R$ is injective and the empty sum is indeed zero. If a normal rank is zero, its Euler denominator is $1$ by [F3], so that term is the ordinary integral over that fixed component. [given, A1, F1, F2, F3, L2, step 1.3]

3.1 If each fixed component is a point $p$, then $\nu_p=T_pM$ has no trivial weight and hence $n=2m$. With the stated ambient normal and positive point orientations, its complex character-line decomposition has Euler class $e_T(T_pM)=\epsilon_p\prod_{a=1}^m u_{\chi_{p,a}}$ by [F1]. Substituting into step 2.1 and using $\epsilon_p^{-1}=\epsilon_p$ yields the isolated-fixed-point formula. If $n=0$, the product is empty and the same calculation reads as the sum of the oriented point evaluations. [given, A1, F1, F3, step 2.1] ∎

