items/thm-atiyah-bott-berline-vergne-integration-formula.md:79:3.1 All maps and identities in [F2] are $\mathcal R$-linear and extend $S^{-1}\mathcal R$-linearly to the localized modules. For a component $F_\ell$, pull back $\gamma$. The $j=\ell$ term is $e_T(\nu_\ell)\,\beta_\ell/e_T(\nu_\ell)=\beta_\ell$ by the localized self-intersection identity; each $j\ne\ell$ term restricts to zero by the localized disjoint-support identity. Hence $i^*\gamma=\beta=i^*\alpha$. Since the localized restriction map in step 1.1 is injective, $\gamma=\alpha$. [given, F2, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]
items/thm-atiyah-bott-berline-vergne-integration-formula.md:81:4.1 The $\mathcal R$-linear pushforwards in [F1] and the identity $\int_M i_{j*}^T(\delta)=\int_{F_j}\delta$ in [F2] extend $S^{-1}\mathcal R$-linearly, so they apply to $\delta=\beta_j/e_T(\nu_j)$. Applying $\int_M$ to step 3.1 gives $\int_M\alpha=\sum_j\int_Mi_{j*}^T(\beta_j/e_T(\nu_j))=\sum_j\int_{F_j}\beta_j/e_T(\nu_j)$, which is the fixed-component formula in the localized coefficient ring. If $F=\varnothing$, [L2] says $S^{-1}H_T^*(M)=0$, so $\int_M\alpha$ localizes to zero; because $\mathcal R$ is a domain and every element of $S$ is nonzero, $\mathcal R\hookrightarrow S^{-1}\mathcal R$ is injective and the empty sum is indeed zero. If a normal rank is zero, its Euler denominator is $1$ by [F3], so that term is the ordinary integral over that fixed component. [given, A1, F1, F2, F3, L2, step 3.1]
items/thm-atiyah-bott-berline-vergne-integration-formula.md:83:5.1 If each fixed component is a point $p$, then $\nu_p=T_pM$ has no trivial weight and hence $n=2m$. With the stated ambient normal and positive point orientations, its complex character-line decomposition has Euler class $e_T(T_pM)=\epsilon_p\prod_{a=1}^m u_{\chi_{p,a}}$ by [F1]. Substituting into step 4.1 and using $\epsilon_p^{-1}=\epsilon_p$ yields the isolated-fixed-point formula. If $n=0$, the product is empty and the same calculation reads as the sum of the oriented point evaluations. [given, A1, F1, F3, step 4.1] ∎
items/ex-abbv-positive-dimensional-fixed-components.md:65:3.1 The dual tautological fiber at $p_0$ has weight $0$ and at $p_\infty$ has weight $-1$, so the first-factor class restricts to $0$ and $-u$. Thus the contribution from $F_0$ is $0/u=0$; on $F_\infty$, the restricted class is $(-u)\eta$ and the normal Euler class is $-u$, leaving $\eta$, whose integral is $1$. The fixed-component sum is therefore $1$. [given, A1, F1, F2, step 1.1, step 2.1]
items/ex-abbv-positive-dimensional-fixed-components.md:67:4.1 Product fiber integration factors over the two compact oriented factors. On the first $\mathbb{CP}^1$, the section of $\mathcal O(1)$ induced by $\ell(z_0,z_1)=z_0$ has one positive transverse zero at $p_\infty$, so its Euler integral is $1$ by the normalized Thom-class zero count; the second-factor integral of $\eta$ is $1$ by definition. Hence $\int_M\alpha=1\cdot1=1$, agreeing with step 3.1. [given, A1, F1, step 3.1] ∎
items/lem-abbv-fixed-component-euler-invertibility.md:69:3.1 If $\nu_j$ has nonzero weights, choose $X\in\mathfrak t$ outside the finite union of their kernels. For a character $\chi$, define the real linear weight $\omega_\chi(X)$ by $\chi(\exp(sX))=\exp(2\pi i s\omega_\chi(X))$. On its real weight subbundle the infinitesimal action $A_X$ satisfies $A_X^2=-(2\pi\omega_\chi(X))^2I$, so $J_\chi=A_X/(2\pi\omega_\chi(X))$ is invariant and satisfies $J_\chi^2=-I$. Their direct sum gives an invariant complex structure on $\nu_j$. Its complex orientation may agree or disagree with the chosen normal orientation, with comparison sign $\epsilon_j$. The weight projections vary continuously, so their ranks, and therefore the character multiplicities, are constant on connected $F_j$. If there are no weights, then $\nu_j=0$ and the complex structure is vacuous. [given, A1, A2, step 2.1, construct]
items/lem-abbv-fixed-component-euler-invertibility.md:71:4.1 The torus acts trivially on $F_j$, hence $ET\times_TF_j\cong BT\times F_j$ and the Künneth isomorphism over $\mathbb R$ gives $H_T^*(F_j;\mathbb R)=H^*(F_j;\mathbb R)\otimes\mathcal R$. Restrict $e_T(\nu_j)$ to any point of $F_j$. The normal representation is the direct sum of the complex character lines in step 3.1; the Euler class of a direct sum is the product because the product of the normalized Thom classes restricts to the positive generator on each direct-sum fiber and hence is its normalized Thom class. By [F1] each line has Euler class $u_{\chi_{j,a}}$, and the orientation comparison gives $f_j=\epsilon_j\prod_a u_{\chi_{j,a}}$ as the degree-zero-in-$H^*(F_j)$ component. Every factor is nonzero and belongs to $S$, so $f_j$ is a unit in $S^{-1}\mathcal R$. [given, A1, F1, F2, step 3.1, algebra]
items/lem-abbv-fixed-component-euler-invertibility.md:73:5.1 Write $e_T(\nu_j)=f_j+n_j$, where $n_j$ has positive degree in $H^*(F_j;\mathbb R)$. Since $F_j$ is compact of finite dimension, every product of sufficiently many positive-degree cohomology classes vanishes; choose $N$ with $n_j^N=0$. In the localized ring the inverse is $f_j^{-1}\sum_{q=0}^{N-1}(-f_j^{-1}n_j)^q$, since multiplying by $f_j+n_j=f_j(1+f_j^{-1}n_j)$ telescopes to $1-(-f_j^{-1}n_j)^N=1$. If $c_j=0$, $n_j=0$ and $f_j=1$, giving the empty-product case as well. [given, A1, step 4.1, algebra] ∎
items/lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities.md:77:3.1 Use the tubular-neighborhood identification and excision to transfer $\tau_\nu$ to $H_T^c(M,M\setminus N;\mathbb R)$. For each $\beta\in H_T^*(N;\mathbb R)$, define $i_*^T(\beta)$ as the image of the cup product of this relative Thom class with the pullback of $\beta$ under the relative-to-absolute map $H_T^*(M,M\setminus N;\mathbb R)\to H_T^*(M;\mathbb R)$. This canonical composite is the Gysin map and is defined for every $\beta$ by the Thom isomorphism. [given, F1, step 1.1, step 2.1, construct]
items/lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities.md:79:4.1 Pull the relative class in step 3.1 back along the zero section. By the definition of $e_T(\nu)$ in [F1], the pulled-back Thom class is $e_T(\nu)$, and pullback respects cup products. Hence $i^*i_*^T(\beta)=e_T(\nu)\smile\beta$. [given, A1, F1, step 3.1, algebra]
items/lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities.md:81:4.2 The Gysin class in step 3.1 is represented in a tubular neighborhood of $N$. Choose such neighborhoods disjoint for disjoint compact fixed components $N,N'$. The representative restricts to zero on $N'$, so $i_{N'}^*i_{N*}^T(\beta)=0$. [given, step 1.1, step 3.1]
items/lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities.md:83:4.3 In the tubular-neighborhood representative from step 3.1, integrate first along each oriented normal disk fiber. The Thom class integrates to $1$ by its fiber normalization, so this fiber integration returns $\beta$ on $N$; the orientation convention in the Statement makes the resulting base orientation the one used by $\int_N$. [given, A1, F1, step 3.1, algebra]
items/lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities.md:85:5.1 Equivariant integration over $M$ factors in the tubular neighborhood into integration over the normal fibers followed by integration over $N$. Step 4.3 therefore gives $\int_M i_*^T(\beta)=\int_N\beta$. These identities establish every stated Gysin property. [given, A1, step 4.1, step 4.3] ∎
items/ex-abbv-projective-line-standard-circle-action.md:69:3.1 Substituting these restrictions and tangent Euler classes in [F2] gives $\int_M x=0/u+(-u)/(-u)=1$. [given, A1, F2, step 1.1, step 2.1]
items/ex-abbv-projective-line-standard-circle-action.md:71:4.1 The linear functional $\ell(z_0,z_1)=z_0$ defines a section of $\mathcal O(1)$ that vanishes only at $p_\infty$. In the local coordinate $v=z_0/z_1$ and the dual tautological frame there, its coefficient is $v$, so its unique zero is transverse with positive local degree $+1$. Pulling the normalized Thom class back by a transverse section counts its isolated zeros with these local degrees: excision reduces the pairing to the degree of the section on the boundary of a small disk around each zero. Hence the ordinary Euler number is $1$. Restricting the Borel pushforward to the basepoint of connected $BT$ gives this ordinary Euler number, and $H^0(BT;\mathbb R)=\mathbb R$; therefore $\int_{\mathbb{CP}^1}x=1$, independently confirming the localized sum. [given, A1, F1, step 2.1] ∎

